## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design scalable, privacy-aware audit pipelines for multi-tenant clusters, balancing compliance with operational overhead.

## Key Concepts
- Kubernetes Audit Logs
- Webhook backends & mutating webhooks
- Per-tenant identity normalization
- Privacy redaction & data retention
- Real-time anomaly detection & alerting

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-processor: route audit events to tenant-scoped store
function routeEvent(evt){
  const tenant = evt.user.tenant;
  store.append(tenant, evt);
  if (detectAnomaly(evt, tenant)) alert(tenant, evt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for false positives at scale?
- Which storage/processing stack would you choose for 1k+ tenants and why?

## Why This Is Asked
A practical beginner-level check for implementing and validating security controls in Kubernetes using policy engines, focusing on least privilege and safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Pod Security Standards Baseline/Restricted
- Policy engines: Kyverno, OPA Gatekeeper
- Privilege escalation controls: runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem, capabilities
- Testing methods: compliant vs non-compliant pods, namespace scoping
- Change management and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Conceptual Kyverno policy (JS object for illustration)
const policy = {
  apiVersion: 'kyverno/v1',
  kind: 'ClusterPolicy',
  metadata: { name: 'require-baseline-security' },
  spec: {
    rules: [
      {
        name: 'require-baseline',
        match: { resources: { kinds: ['Pod'] }},
        validate: {
          message: 'Pods must set runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem and avoid hostPath',
          pattern: {
            spec: {
              containers: { any: { securityContext: { runAsNonRoot: true, readOnlyRootFilesystem: true } }},
              hostPath: { absent: {} }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle exceptions for legitimate hostPath usage?
- How would you monitor for policy violations in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Addresses multi-cloud data governance, tenancy, and compliance through policy-as-code, ensuring data privacy, residency, and auditable CI validation in production-scale ML platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Data masking in logs/telemetry
- Per-tenant data residency and envelope encryption
- Tenant-aware RBAC across clouds
- Policy-as-code (OPA/Kyverno) + CI gates

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactPII(logEntry) {
  const redacted = { ...logEntry };
  for (const k of Object.keys(redacted)) {
    if (k.toLowerCase().includes('ssn') || k.toLowerCase().includes('email')) {
      redacted[k] = 'REDACTED';
    }
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test fail-closed masking in CI across multiple tenants?
- What metrics would you surface to detect residency violations at runtime?

## Why This Is Asked

Introduces a practical policy gate that enforces manifest hygiene early in CI, ensuring traceability and consistency across deployments.

## Key Concepts

- YAML manifest traversal and metadata fields
- Distinguishing annotations vs labels and required keys
- Lightweight Node.js validator using js-yaml
- Integrating validator into GitHub Actions as a PR gate

## Code Example

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const yaml = require('js-yaml');
function valid(manifest) {
  const m = yaml.load(fs.readFileSync(manifest, 'utf8'));
  const meta = m?.metadata || {};
  const ann = meta.annotations || {};
  const lab = meta.labels || {};
  return ann['app.kubernetes.io/managed-by'] === 'ci-tool' && lab.app && lab.version;
}
module.exports = { valid };
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend the validator to report which manifests fail and why?
- How would you adapt the workflow to run in parallel across multiple directories?
